PLACES=[
N('eldridge-house-hotel-lawrence-ks','Eldridge House Hotel','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','hotel','standing',38.97107,-95.23613,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1856, rebuilt 1925',
  'The hotel traces its origin to the Free State Hotel, a way station for antislavery settlers that was burned in the Sack of Lawrence on May 21, 1856. Shalor Eldridge then built the Eldridge House, which Quantrill\'s raiders burned on August 21, 1863. A third hotel was finished in 1866, and the present building dates from a rebuilding in 1925. It stopped being a hotel in the late 1960s, became apartments on July 1, 1970, was made a hotel again in the late 1980s, and was bought and renovated in 2004 to restore its 1925 look; it reopened in 2005.',
  'A downtown hotel building on the corner of Seventh and Massachusetts. It is a working hotel with a restaurant, the Eldridge House Grille (its own site). What original fabric survives inside was not checked.',
  'A hotel burned in 1856 and again in 1863, and still a hotel.',
  'A working hotel on Massachusetts Street; the lobby and restaurant are used by guests and diners. No opening times are given on the hotel\'s home page, so hours are not checked. Metered street parking and city lots downtown.',
  [W('The Eldridge Hotel'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%3],['The Eldridge Hotel','https://www.eldridgehotel.com/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('douglas-county-courthouse-lawrence-ks','Douglas County Courthouse','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.96344,-95.23552,'exact','view','drive','','1903',
  'A three-and-a-half-story stone courthouse built in 1903, designed by John G. Haskell with Frederick C. Gunn in the Richardsonian Romanesque style. Its main feature is a six-story square clock tower with four minarets and a pyramidal roof, with a smaller octagonal stair tower beside it. It was listed on the National Register on April 14, 1975.',
  'The clock tower and the octagonal stair tower on Massachusetts Street at 11th. Wikipedia says only that the building is of stone; the kind of stone and the interior were not checked.',
  '',
  'A county building at 11th and Massachusetts, in downtown Lawrence. Look at it from the street; opening hours of its offices were not checked. Metered street parking nearby.',
  [W('Douglas County Courthouse (Kansas)'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%3]],
  brick='no'),
N('watkins-museum-old-city-hall-lawrence-ks','Watkins Museum of History (Watkins National Bank and old city hall)','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.96401,-95.23613,'exact','open','drive','','1885–1888',
  'A Romanesque bank building at 11th and Massachusetts, built for Watkins National Bank (the Register summary dates it 1885 to 1888; Wikipedia says the bank opened in 1888). The bank lasted until 1929. Watkins\'s widow Elizabeth gave the building to the city, which used it as city hall from 1929 to 1970. After renovations it reopened in 1975 as a museum, which is now run by the Douglas County Historical Society. It was listed on the National Register on February 24, 1971; the architects are given as Cobb and Frost.',
  'Three floors of exhibits on Lawrence and Douglas County. The museum says the third floor holds a 1920s Milburn electric car and a desk used by James Naismith, inventor of basketball. Admission is free (Wikipedia; not stated on the museum\'s visit page). Next door is a Japanese friendship garden.',
  'A bank that became city hall and then the county\'s museum.',
  'Run by the Douglas County Historical Society. Metered street parking in front on Massachusetts Street and on 11th Street (museum\'s visit page).',
  [['Watkins Museum: plan your visit','https://watkinsmuseum.org/visit/'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%13],W('Lawrence, Kansas')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('carnegie-library-ninth-vermont-lawrence-ks','Carnegie Library at Ninth and Vermont (old Lawrence public library)','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.96773,-95.2374,'exact','view','drive','','1904',
  'Andrew Carnegie gave $27,500 for a free public library on condition that the city keep it up; the Classical Revival building by George A. Berlinghof of Beatrice, Nebraska opened to the public on December 26, 1904, on land given by Mrs. Charles Grosvenor. It was the city\'s main library until a new one opened at Seventh and Vermont in 1972. The old building is on the National Register (1975).',
  'The Classical Revival front on the northwest corner of Ninth and Vermont. What the building is used for now was not confirmed: Wikipedia says the Friends of the Library took it over in 1972, and one directory listing calls it an arts-centre building. The interior was not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Lawrence as of its 2005 call for proposals for the building\'s use; who uses it now was not checked. Look from the sidewalk. Metered street parking.',
  [W('Lawrence Public Library'),['The Carnegie Library, 1904 to 1937 (Lawrence Public Library history)','https://history.lplks.org/exhibits/show/library-history/carnegie'],['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%8]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('plymouth-congregational-church-lawrence-ks','Plymouth Congregational Church','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',38.96658,-95.23731,'exact','view','drive','','1868–1870',
  'The congregation was founded in 1854, months after Kansas Territory opened, and held its first service on October 1, 1854 in a mudbrick boarding house. Its first permanent building, the "Stone Church", was in use from 1857 and finished in 1862. The present church, also called the Brick Church, was designed by John G. Haskell, begun in 1868 and finished in May 1870 at a cost of $45,000. Services have been held in it regularly since 1870. The Lawrence massacre of 1863 killed several members.',
  'The 1870 church on Vermont Street. Wikipedia says its sanctuary windows were made in England. The inside was not checked, and the church\'s own pages could not be read for service times.',
  '',
  'An active church. Times of services were not found on a page that could be read; a search result suggested a 9:30 a.m. Sunday service, which is not confirmed. Metered street parking downtown.',
  [W('Plymouth Congregational Church (Lawrence, Kansas)'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%9]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('spooner-hall-ku-lawrence-ks','Spooner Hall, University of Kansas','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',38.95854,-95.24759,'exact','view','drive','','1893–1894',
  'The University of Kansas\'s first library building, designed by Henry Van Brunt in the Richardsonian Romanesque style and paid for by a bequest from William B. Spooner, a Massachusetts leather merchant. It is built of rough-faced gray Oread limestone quarried on Mount Oread, with red Dakota sandstone on the corners, bands and sills. A new library replaced it in 1924, it became the Spooner-Thayer Museum of Art in 1926, and the art collection moved to the Spencer Museum in 1978. It holds anthropology collections and serves as a conference center. Listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'A steep gabled clay-tile roof, a sculpted owl on the west gable, and heavy rough-faced limestone. Wikipedia says the original interior was entirely changed for use as a gallery.',
  '',
  'A building on the University of Kansas campus; whether visitors may go in was not checked. Look from the campus walks. Campus parking is mostly by permit; the university\'s visitor parking rules were not checked.',
  [W('Spooner Hall'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%11]],
  brick='no'),
N('dyche-hall-natural-history-museum-lawrence-ks','Dyche Hall (University of Kansas Natural History Museum)','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',38.95858,-95.24373,'exact','open','drive','','1901–1903',
  'Dyche Hall was built between 1901 and 1903 to house the Panorama of North American Wildlife, part of which stood for Kansas at the 1893 World\'s Columbian Exposition in Chicago. It was designed by Root and Siemens in the Romanesque style, with walls of Oread limestone and carved window facings, columns, arches and grotesques of Cottonwood limestone. It is named for the naturalist Lewis Lindsay Dyche and was listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'The building is modeled on a French church, with the Panorama in the "apse" of the entrance gallery. The museum says it has four floors of public exhibits including the Panorama, fossils and live snakes and insects; Wikipedia adds Comanche, the horse that survived the Little Bighorn, and one of three Victory Eagle statues in Kansas.',
  'A museum built as a church-shaped case for a diorama.',
  'A University of Kansas museum. Hours are from the museum\'s visit page; admission was not checked. Campus parking is mostly by permit; visitor parking was not checked.',
  [['KU Natural History Museum: visit','https://nhm.ku.edu/visit'],W('University of Kansas Natural History Museum'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%3]],
  brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('haskell-institute-campus-lawrence-ks','Haskell Institute historic campus (Hiawatha Hall, Tecumseh Hall and others)','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',38.9386,-95.2333,'area','view','drive','','1884–1930s',
  'Haskell opened in 1884 as one of the first large federal boarding schools for Native American children, and is now Haskell Indian Nations University. The Register summary says no buildings remain from 1884 to 1894 except the school cemetery; five date from 1895 to 1915 and six from the 1920s to the mid-1930s. It is a National Historic Landmark (July 4, 1961) and was listed on the Register on October 15, 1966. Wikipedia says Hiawatha Hall, built in 1898 and dedicated in 1899, has been closed for decades for lack of repair money, and that Tecumseh Hall was built in 1915 as a gymnasium and Pocahontas Hall in 1931.',
  'The landmark buildings are spread over a working campus; the summary lists an archway, auditorium, bandstand, stadium, Hiawatha, Kiva, Pocahontas, Powhatan, Pushmataha and Tecumseh halls, the old dairy and the Indian cemetery. A medicine wheel earthwork built in 1992 lies south of the campus, and the Haskell Cultural Center and Museum is on campus.',
  '',
  'Federal land and a working university. Which buildings the public may enter, and when, was not checked: the university\'s page could not be opened. Be respectful of the cemetery and of classes. The pin is on the middle of the historic campus; individual buildings were not located.',
  [W('Haskell Indian Nations University'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%5]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('oak-hill-cemetery-lawrence-ks','Oak Hill Cemetery','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','burial','standing',38.95806,-95.21468,'site','view','drive','cemeteries','1866',
  'The cemetery was begun in 1866, after Quantrill\'s raid of 1863, partly as a memorial to the citizens killed and partly to show that Lawrence was a settled city. Holland Wheeler laid it out in the rural-cemetery style, and the Register form calls it a masterful example in the Midwest. People buried here include the free-state leaders Charles L. Robinson and James H. Lane, John P. Usher, the dentist Lucy Hobbs Taylor, and Charles and Mary Langston, the grandparents of Langston Hughes. It was listed on the National Register in 2017.',
  'A designed cemetery landscape with winding drives and old monuments. Which stones mark raid victims was not checked.',
  '',
  'A cemetery at 1605 Oak Hill Avenue, which Wikipedia and the Register summary do not say is owned by the city, so the owner was not checked; the cemetery\'s hours were not checked, and cemeteries are normally open in daylight. Park inside on the drives or on the street, and keep to the paths.',
  [W('Oak Hill Cemetery (Lawrence, Kansas)'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%8]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('reuter-organ-buildings-lawrence-ks','Reuter Organ Company buildings','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','industry','standing',38.97268,-95.23444,'exact','view','drive','','1882, 1927',
  'The stone building at 612 New Hampshire Street was put up in 1882 and housed the Wilder Brothers Shirt Factory until 1912. The Reuter Organ Company bought it in 1919 and remodeled it for organ making, then built the brick building at 614 to 616 in 1927. The firm was headquartered here from 1919 to 2001. Both buildings were listed on the National Register in 2020.',
  'A stone factory with a later brick one beside it in the east Lawrence downtown. Whether the buildings are in use now was not checked.',
  '',
  'Look from the sidewalk on New Hampshire Street; the present owner and use were not checked. Metered street parking and city lots nearby.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%9]],
  conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('santa-fe-depot-lawrence-ks','Santa Fe Depot (Lawrence Amtrak station)','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','rail','standing',38.9713,-95.23041,'exact','view','drive','stations','1956',
  'The Santa Fe built this depot to replace an 1883 station damaged by the Great Flood of 1951. The old building was torn down starting April 15, 1955, and the new one opened February 7, 1956. It is Mid-Century Modern in style, designed by Warren Corman and Warren Jones, and the Santa Fe built similar stations elsewhere in Kansas. It was added to the National Register in 2018, and Amtrak\'s Southwest Chief still calls here.',
  'A low 1950s station with a canopy and neon signs reading "Lawrence"; Wikipedia says the neon was replaced in a 2011 project. A photograph on Wikimedia Commons shows buff brick walls and large windows. Inside was not checked.',
  '',
  'An active Amtrak stop at 413 East Seventh Street; waiting-room hours were not checked. Parking at the station was not checked.',
  [W('Lawrence station (Kansas)'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%10]],
  brick='no'),
N('double-hyperbolic-paraboloid-house-lawrence-ks','Double Hyperbolic Paraboloid House','Lawrence, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.94665,-95.24674,'exact','view','drive','','1956',
  'Donald L. Dean, then an associate professor of civil engineering at the University of Kansas, designed this house in 1956. The Register summary describes a saddle-shaped surface whose low points form the supports. The Register summary says it cost $18,000 partly furnished and that Fortune magazine in February 1957 found it had as much finished floor as a $36,000 conventional house. It was listed on the National Register in 2007.',
  'A house with a saddle-shaped roof, on West 21st Street. Its materials were not checked.',
  'A house shaped like a potato chip.',
  'A private house, almost certainly lived in: look from the street and do not go onto the property. The owner was not checked.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%3]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('holy-family-catholic-church-eudora-ks','Old Holy Family Catholic Church (Adoration Chapel), Eudora','Eudora, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',38.94308,-95.09561,'exact','view','drive','','',
  'The Register summary calls Holy Family one of the oldest structures in Eudora, a Gothic Revival church tied to the German settlement that began the town, and says church records show it is the oldest Catholic church still standing and in use in Kansas. It was listed on the National Register in 2020. The summary gives no building date, and the claim comes from the parish records as the summary quotes them: no second source was read.',
  'Photographs on Wikimedia Commons show a church of native stone with a square bell tower, pointed Gothic windows and a shingled roof. OpenStreetMap maps this building as "Old Holy Family Catholic Church - Adoration Chapel" and a newer church of the parish a block away, so the old one may now serve as a chapel; that was not confirmed. The inside was not checked.',
  '',
  'Part of an active parish. The parish\'s site could not be opened, so no hours are given; directories disagree on addresses and times. The Register lists it at 911 East Ninth Street; the pin is on the stone church shown in photographs, about 150 m from the Register\'s coordinate. Street parking.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%5]],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('cohn-gardner-hill-store-eudora-ks','Cohn/Gardner-Hill and Company Store','Eudora, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.94602,-95.09861,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1860, c. 1880',
  'A two-story wood-frame commercial building on Main Street in the middle of Eudora\'s downtown, put up in two stages, about 1860 and about 1880. The Register summary says part of the northwest section keeps the frame, sheathing and some interior features of a smaller Territorial-period building (1854 to 1861). It was listed on the National Register in 2021.',
  'A wooden storefront, rare among the stone and brick buildings of eastern Kansas. What it is used for today was not checked.',
  '',
  'Look from Main Street. The owner and any hours were not checked. Street parking.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%3]],
  brick='no'),
N('beni-israel-cemetery-eudora-ks','Beni Israel Cemetery','Eudora, Kansas','ks','burial','standing',38.92821,-95.11253,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1858',
  'Founded in 1858 by German and Polish Jews of the settlement that founded Eudora, it is the only surviving place tied to Eudora\'s Jewish community, which flourished from 1857 to 1867 before moving to Lawrence. The first burial was one-year-old Isaac Cohn, who died September 5, 1858. Burials continued until the late 1920s, and in 1978 the Lawrence Jewish Community Congregation took over its care. It was listed on the National Register in 2013.',
  'A small country burial ground; stones carry early Eudora Jewish family names such as Urbansky, Cohn and Katzenstein.',
  '',
  'Kept by the Lawrence Jewish Community Congregation (Wikipedia). On East 2100 Road. Visiting rules and hours were not checked; treat it as a working cemetery and keep to the roadside unless the congregation says visitors are welcome.',
  [W('Beni Israel Cemetery'),['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%1]],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('barnes-apple-barn-vinland-ks','Barnes Apple Barn','Vinland, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.84274,-95.18181,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1857 (or 1891)',
  'A gabled limestone building with a metal roof, put up about 1857 to process grapes, apples and pears from the Vinland Nursery and Fruit Farm. The caption of a 2025 photograph of the same farmstead on Wikimedia Commons says the apple barn was built in 1891, so the date is not settled. William E. Barnes, a founder of Vinland, started the first vineyard in Kansas. It was listed on the National Register in 2006.',
  'A plain stone farm building on 1728 Road, near the Coal Creek Library and the Vinland Presbyterian church. Its present use was not checked.',
  '',
  'The land belongs to a private owner whose name was not checked: look from the road. Park on the road shoulder.',
  [['Kansas Historical Society register summary',KSNR%1],W('Vinland, Kansas')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
]
